      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">Linear axes zoomed to f_n 0.5 .. 2.25 - the sweep runs wider than that and the pane clips it - and four legend entries each carrying its own peak. Lighter load is the taller curve: Q = 0.2 peaks at 4.67, Q = 0.4 peaks at 2.41, Q = 0.766 peaks at 1.42, Q =</p>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">1.5 peaks at 1.08.</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="2574" width="753" height="325" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <zedgraph width="743" height="317">
        <data encoding="base64"></data>
        <input>
          <e type="operand">P.a</e>
          <e type="operand">P.b</e>
          <e type="operand">P.c</e>
          <e type="operand">P.d</e>
          <e type="operand">4</e>
          <e type="operand">1</e>
          <e type="function" args="6">sys</e>
        </input>
      </zedgraph>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="2988" width="378" height="26" color="#000000" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 12px;">6  the same four in dB, on a logarithmic f_n axis</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="3024" width="1016" height="21" color="#000000" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">The other way this family is drawn - dB against a log f_n, both axes as a filter response would have them. The log axis is labelled the same way as section 7. Peaks in dB: 13.4, 7.6, 3.1, 0.7.</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="3051" width="562" height="325" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <zedgraph width="552" height="317">
        <data encoding="base64"></data>
        <input>
          <e type="operand">B.a</e>
          <e type="operand">B.b</e>
          <e type="operand">B.c</e>
          <e type="operand">B.d</e>
          <e type="operand">4</e>
          <e type="operand">1</e>
          <e type="function" args="6">sys</e>
        </input>
      </zedgraph>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="3474" width="521" height="26" color="#000000" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 12px;">7  THE INFINEON PICTURE      linear gain, log f_n, two full decades</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="3510" width="1384" height="49" color="#000000" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">0.1 to 10 on a logarithmic x, the way the Infineon guide draws this family. Two whole decades means the major ticks land on 0.1 and 1 and 10 and each decade carries the fixed 1, 2 ... 9 minor mesh, so the frame reads at a glance without a single odd number</p>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">on it - which is the point of choosing the span to be whole decades.</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="3564" width="1164" height="21" color="#000000" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">Section 5 is the same four curves on a linear x zoomed to 0.5 .. 2.25. The log frame spends 50 % of its width below resonance where every peak sits; the linear frame spends 29 % but shows nothing outside the zoom.</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="3600" width="562" height="325" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <zedgraph width="552" height="317">
        <data encoding="base64"></data>
        <input>
          <e type="operand">P.a</e>
          <e type="operand">P.b</e>
          <e type="operand">P.c</e>
          <e type="operand">P.d</e>
          <e type="operand">4</e>
          <e type="operand">1</e>
          <e type="function" args="6">sys</e>
        </input>
      </zedgraph>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4014" width="456" height="26" color="#000000" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 12px;">8  full load alone      the single-curve pane, for comparison</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4050" width="745" height="21" color="#000000" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">Expected: a hump reaching 1.42 near f_n 0.69, crossing 1.00 exactly at f_n = 1, and falling to 0.50 at the right edge of the frame, f_n 2.25.</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4077" width="562" height="325" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <zedgraph width="552" height="317">
        <data encoding="base64"></data>
        <input>
          <e type="operand">P.c</e>
        </input>
      </zedgraph>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4455" width="570" height="26" color="#000000" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 12px;">9  HOW MANY TRACES WILL A PANE TAKE      count the horizontal lines</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4491" width="1347" height="49" color="#000000" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">Every pane in this project so far was fed exactly as many matrices as it was lifted with. This asks what happens otherwise, and the gain figure in the design guide depends on the answer: if a pane builds its curves from the input then six load curves and</p>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">two marker lines fit in ONE frame, the way the Infineon guide draws them. If it only refills the curves it already holds, four is the ceiling whenever the y axis has to stay linear, and the figure has to be split.</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4545" width="1380" height="49" color="#000000" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">Each trace is a horizontal line at its own height, so there is nothing to judge - count them. T1 gives a FOUR-curve pane EIGHT lines: eight visible means any number works, four means the pane is the limit, none or red means it refuses. T2 gives a ONE-curve</p>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">pane THREE. T3 goes the other way and gives a FOUR-curve pane only TWO, which matters just as much - a pane that will not draw when under-fed cannot be used for a small family.</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4608" width="143" height="23" color="#000000" fontSize="10" isBreakable="false">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 10px;">[PICK] points per line:</p>
      </content>
      </text>
    </region>
